comments[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-23T11:55:15.946Z","harness:claude-code","Scope addition from the third Codex pass on PR #1772 (2026-11-23, not reproduced here): when a command declares both --many and --no-many as repeatable switches, --many --no-Many reaches the handler as [false, true] for many instead of [true, false]. Commander leaves the mixed-case token unparsed and the loose parser records it as many=false, but the ordering scan folds --no-Many to the noMany key, so its slot count disagrees and the loose-first fallback applies (no value is lost, the order is). The fix belongs with the position-based merge: assign each loose slot with the loose parser's own negation rule (a --no- prefix inverts the base key) rather than the folded key.\n"
body: ""
